Here is the simple script using `node` as the server instead of `nginx`.
I disabled the `keep-alive` to simulate the problem, you can also change to `75` which match the nginx default.

You can see that it never been able to close without consuming the payload, or early than the keep-alive timeout.
From your case, the `160kb` seems to works because the payload is split into two part (by nginx). So, the content never received by fetch and actually considered as consumed all payload (status code and headers).
the `40kb` case is that the status, headers and payload is inside a single chunk. So, `fetch` requires to consume the later part of it.

```js
import { createServer } from 'http';
import { setDefaultHighWaterMark } from 'stream';
import { fetch } from 'undici';
const start = performance.now()

setDefaultHighWaterMark(false, 16 * 1024)
const kb = new Array(1024).fill('0').join('')

const server = createServer((request, response) => {
  response.setHeader('content-type', 'text/plain')
  switch (request.url) {
    case '/40kb.txt': {
      response.setHeader('content-length', '40960')
      for (let i = 0; i < 40; i++) {
        response.write(kb)
      }
      break
    }
    case '/160kb.txt': {
      response.setHeader('content-length', '163840')
      for (let i = 0; i < 40; i++) {
        response.write(kb)
      }
      break
    }
  }
  response.end()
})
server.keepAliveTimeout = 0
server.listen(3000, '127.0.0.1')

// 40kb
await fetch(`http://127.0.0.1:3000/40kb.txt`);
// 160kb
// await fetch(`http://127.0.0.1:3000/160kb.txt`);
server.close()

process.on('beforeExit', () => {
  const end = performance.now()
  console.log(`Script ended in %s ms`, end - start)
})
```

