Ah, yes, in that case this is unsupported and not an error we should handle. If a service needs to handle such invalid cases then they can pass their own content type parser:

https://www.fastify.io/docs/latest/Reference/Server/#addcontenttypeparser
```js
'use strict'

const fastify = require('fastify')({ log: { level: 'debug' } })

fastify.addContentTypeParser(
  'text/plain, charset=utf-8',
  { asString: true },
  fastify.defaultTextParser
)

fastify.route({
  path: '/foo',
  method: 'POST',
  async handler (req, reply) {
    let result = ''
    for await (const line of req.body) {
      // Note: it was unexpected that `req.body` is a readable stream
      // this feels like a bug
      result += line
    }

    return reply.send({
      received: result
    })
  }
})

fastify.listen({ port: 3000 })
```

```sh
❯ curl -d 'foo bar' -H 'content-type: text/plain, charset=utf-8' 127.0.0.1:3000/foo
{"received":"foo bar"}
```

Thanks for the suggestion! I've tried it, but at least injecting another service holding `JhiLanguageService` did not do the trick. I've noticed though, that the same cycle seems to be resolved with JHipster 6.2.0, so I somehow suggest it is something that used to be more "lazy" and now kicks in earlier (changes on `language-helper` removing the workaround with the observable maybe?)

However I've found one way to fix it: Make the resolution of `JhiLanguageService` within `AccountService` lazy, i.e. change the constructor

```
private lazyLanguageService;

constructor(private injector: Injector, private sessionStorage: SessionStorageService, private http: HttpClient) {
  this.lazyLanguageService = () => injector.get(JhiLanguageService)
}
```

and then dynamically resolve `JhiLanguageService` where it is needed:

```
if (this.userIdentity.langKey) {
  const langKey = this.sessionStorage.retrieve('locale') || this.userIdentity.langKey;
  this.lazyLanguageService().changeLanguage(langKey);
}
```

Anyway, I don't like this solution a lot, because it changes JHipster generated code, so we will have merge conflicts during the next update. Thus I'd still be happy about a clean solution!

@vishal423 thanks for the answer and the tips. I wasn't exactly sure, how I would apply your suggestion about `defaultI18nLang`. We don't want to "force" a default language on the user, but take the user's browser language as default for our application.
The config containing `defaultI18nLang` goes into `NgJhipsterModule` very early (module import of `CoreModule`), and since I need to inject `TranslateService` to get the browser language, it seems impossible to manipulate the language there.

What I tried now, and what seems to work, is to inject `JhiConfigService` into the constructor of `AppModule` and then changing `configService.getConfig().defaultI18nLang`. Is this how you would imagine it? I couldn't find any config object I could directly inject, since `JhiModuleConfig` is not the same object that is used by the `JhiConfigService`.

@kaidohallik thanks for your analysis effort! To me the behavior seems to be somewhat strange. Even though resource bundles should not be protected, as @vishal423 pointed out, it seems evident that the interceptors are instantiated and injected when the `TranslateService` makes its GET request.
I agree that simply setting the `languageService`, if it is undefined, is more efficient. However, I found when you have to add this guard to multiple places, it affects maintainability, so I would always rather use something like a "memoizing supplier" (callback that fetches the value only during the first call). Since the class is pretty small and the check only needs to be performed in one place, it doesn't make much difference though :wink:

I was able to make HTTP interceptors DI process stable after I removed `init()` from `JhiLanguageService` constructor https://github.com/jhipster/ng-jhipster/blob/master/src/language/language.service.ts#L31 and put that into generated app constructor.  
@codecholeric thanks for providing useful information for that enhancement.  
Providing PR-s to `ng-jhipster` and `generator-jhipster` (this needs new release of the `ng-jhipster`) to fix this issue.

@tomekzaw I'm still seeing this in 3.0.0-rc3 for RN 0.70.4

From what I can tell this is due to the following
```
def isDeveloperMode() {
  // developer mode, to run Example app                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             
  return file("$projectDir/../node_modules/react-native/package.json").exists()                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     
}   

 def resolveReactNativeDirectory() {                        
   if (isDeveloperMode()) {                               
     return file("$projectDir/../${getPlaygroundAppName()}/node_modules/react-native")
   }                                                                                                   
   return file("$projectDir/../../react-native")
}
```

When ```isDeveloperMode``` returns ```true``` then ```resolveReactNativeDirectory``` injects the app name, in my case ```mobile``` into the path.

If I hard code ```isDeveloperMode``` to ```false```, everything builds fine.

Does ```isDeveloperMode``` exist just for the example app, or is it supposed to be true for dev builds?

The issue is reproducible without third party libraries. Attaching a jQuery `focus` handler on an element makes native handlers attached later not fire at all:
```html
<input id="input">
<button id="button">Run the test</button>
```
```js
var $button = $("#button");
var $input = $('#input');
var input = $input[0];

input.addEventListener('focus', function () {
	console.log('native focus handler 1');
});

$input.on("focus", function () {
	console.log("jQuery focus handler");
});

input.addEventListener('focus', function () {
	console.log('native focus handler 2');
});

$("#button").on("click", function () {
	$input.trigger("focus");
});
```
Only `native focus handler 1` & `jQuery focus handler` will be logged.

Test case: https://jsbin.com/wupovat/1/edit?html,js,console,output

The reason is most likely the `event.stopImmediatePropagation()` call at the end of this block:
https://github.com/jquery/jquery/blob/cff2899885c314d32eea42e9eef6ead6e5da5c2f/src/event.js#L580-L598

This issue *is not* fixed by my PR #5223 which otherwise fixes a number of other `focus` issues.

