But `qs` is usually a nested dependency so there is no way to override `arrayLimit` because qs.parse is called somewhere in third party code. Also there is no way to change defaults for `qs` and even if there was one different libs can depend on different versions of `qs` so there is no easy way to set defaults for all.

I see 2 possible solutions:
1. Raise default from `20` to `100` or even bigger (original [commit](https://github.com/ljharb/qs/commit/4667c41c586ba2bca3c2fdc8c7cc362587ddeeae) doesn't have any justification for value). Not a solid solution because there might still be people who need bigger value.
2. (solid) Add a way to override it globally via env var (making sure it works in non-node.js runtimes or at least doesn't fail)
```js

  function getEnvVar (name) {
      try {
          // Node.js / Bun
          if (typeof process !== 'undefined' && process.env) {
              return process.env[name];
          }
          // Deno
          if (typeof Deno !== 'undefined' && Deno.env && typeof Deno.env.get === 'function') {
              return Deno.env.get(name);
          }
      } catch (e) {
          // Deno throws without --allow-env permission, ignore
          // can probably log a warning
      }
      return undefined;
  }

  function parseEnvInt (val, fallback) {
      if (val === undefined || val === null || val === '') {
          return fallback;
      }
      var parsed = parseInt(val, 10);
      return isNaN(parsed) ? fallback : parsed;
  };

  var defaults = {
      arrayLimit: parseEnvInt(getEnvVar('QS_ARRAY_LIMIT'), 20),
      depth: parseEnvInt(getEnvVar('QS_DEPTH'), 5),
      parameterLimit: parseEnvInt(getEnvVar('QS_PARAMETER_LIMIT'), 1000),
      // ... other options use hardcoded defaults
  };
```

So it will be used like `QS_ARRAY_LIMIT=100 node app.js`

I can provide a PR for solution 2

## Historical background

I tried looking for historical changes related to parsing arrays and `arrayLimit` in the repository.

The first versions of `qs` NPM package (<1.0.0) were developed as [visionmedia/node-querystring](https://github.com/visionmedia/node-querystring) (now [tj/node-querystring](https://github.com/tj/node-querystring)). It supported arrays from the beginning with no limits, as it was supposed to replace the built-in `querystring` module that lost its array/object parsing functionality in 0.3.0 (https://github.com/nodejs/node/commit/422d3c93bc7391e105cfb4363011088c27ec86a6).

Then the current `qs` appeared. I believe that it started as [`nlf/riddler`](https://github.com/ljharb/qs/blob/e3daf923d341419a9f240ad8d390021e46e67b7a/package.json), then became `spumko/riddler` (https://github.com/ljharb/qs/commit/aad2a70068e7cb670a99e0ed8b55057764111d60) and on 2014-08-01 `hapi/riddler` (https://github.com/ljharb/qs/commit/17b1fdce68f895366c65c47b9fb3106c67a224f7). Finally, on 2014-08-05 the project was renamed to `hapi/qs` (https://github.com/ljharb/qs/commit/2007b86a4bc1ab6d2701e04daed07af9e1e8da28) and at version [1.0.0](https://www.npmjs.com/package/qs/v/1.0.0) released to NPM, replacing TJ's `qs`. Everything that follows is related to this repository, which is now (since 2015-12-23) `ljharb/qs` (https://github.com/ljharb/qs/commit/f42b8fc07422e56cf483f6c8a65d1ba3925264e1), but it may have happened when others were maintaining it.

## Timeline

_All times are in UTC._

1. 2014-07-25 18:35: First commit, I'm not completely sure about array parsing support - https://github.com/ljharb/qs/commit/b1e7b5386d31bbef9f51e1c01c61673524c871aa
2. 2014-07-25 19:14: Sixth commit, introducing a _simple array_ parsing test `a=b&a=c` => `['b', 'c']` - https://github.com/ljharb/qs/commit/622da20bd65f58edeba784ba296d62dc94bbbdf9
3. 2014-07-31 17:14: A hardcoded limit **to explicitly specified array indices** is introduced (max 20) - https://github.com/ljharb/qs/commit/4667c41c586ba2bca3c2fdc8c7cc362587ddeeae
4. 2014-07-31 17:16: The limit is documented in readme, mentions _specifying indices_ and is in the section about using indices in array notation - https://github.com/ljharb/qs/commit/8c5cda39eaf44ebbe2989011830ef4708e8eac5f
5. 2014-08-13 14:18: First issue asking about `arrayLimit` value and reason. Answer: it is required for security and should be low - https://github.com/ljharb/qs/issues/18
6. 2014-08-25 20:01: Limits are made configurable - https://github.com/ljharb/qs/pull/21
7. 2015-06-10 05:59: Another question about `arrayLimit`. Last 2 comments suggest that too large index may cause an exception or exceed callstack size (how?) - https://github.com/ljharb/qs/issues/95
8. 2019-01-09 20:40: **IMPORTANT** @Jokero asks why `arrayLimit` applies only to arrays with explicit indices. @ljharb explains that the problem occurs only when someone can specify an arbitrary index and **it is not a problem without brackets** - https://github.com/ljharb/qs/issues/294
9. 2019-01-21 04:21: A clarification is added to readme that explicitly mentions large user/attacker-specified indices as the problem solved by `arrayLimit` - https://github.com/ljharb/qs/pull/296
10. 2025-12-29 22:53: GHSA-6rw7-vpxm-498p is published with question almost identical to #294, but this time it is considered a vulnerability
11. 2026-01-07 17:04: @kroleg notices that a DOS is impossible if the attacker cannot specify index, because the largest array is limited to the size of largest object anyway (and proves this with benchmarks) - https://github.com/ljharb/qs/issues/539

## My opinion

GHSA-6rw7-vpxm-498p is not a vulnerability, because `arrayLimit` was (historically) intended to limit the maximal explicit index and processing arrays is usually faster than objects of the same size. Because of those historical reasons, I don't think it is a bug, rather a (questionable) design decision. This is also what was decided in #294 almost exactly 7 years ago.

Some (non-scientific) timing-only (the vulnerability is supposedly "memory exhaustion") benchmarks, based on code below (single run, 100k elements, limits are modified - see code):

| What (who) | data type | 6.14.0 (ms) | 6.14.1 (ms) |
| - | - | - | - |
| parse (qs) | array | 30326 | 30254 |
| parse (qs) | object | - | 55 |
| process (user) | array | 4.6 | 4.6 |
| process (user) | object | - | 17 |

**EDIT:** With 6.14.0 I can't parse repeating keys as an object with `parseArrays: false`, be it `a=b&a=b` (`{ a: [ 'b', 'b' ] }`) or `a[]=b&a[]=b` (`{ a: { '0': [ 'b', 'b' ] } }`). 6.14.1 ~~fixes this bug~~ changes this. See #543 for more details.

I tried it also with explicit indices and then (regardless of version) parsing arrays takes **~88 s (full seconds!)** and objects ~200 ms. If the `parameterLimit` is kept as default, then most of this does not matter, but _infinite_ (or very large) `arrayLimit` will make the parsing ~2 slower than objects in 6.14.1 and execution time still scales with input length when `parameterLimit` is constant (https://github.com/ljharb/qs/issues/539#issuecomment-3732921020).

<details>
<summary>Benchmark code</summary>

```js
const {
  timerify,
  performance,
  PerformanceObserver,
} = require("node:perf_hooks");
const qs = require(".");

const N = 100_000;
const query = Array(N).fill("a[]=b").join("&");

function parseArray() {
    return qs.parse(query, {
        parameterLimit: N,
        arrayLimit: N
    });
}
const timedParseArray = timerify(parseArray);

function parseObject() {
    return qs.parse(query, {
        parameterLimit: N,
        arrayLimit: -1,
        parseArrays: false
    });
}
const timedParseObject = timerify(parseObject);

function processElement(index, value) {
    // index is a string in case of objects, but number for arrays, but whatever
    // real code will also have differences depending on the type of parsed "array"
    return `${index}: ${value}`
}

function processArray(arr) {
    for (const [i, e] of arr.entries()) {
        arr[i] = processElement(i, e);
    }
}
const timedProcessArray = timerify(processArray);

function processObject(obj) {
    for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(obj)) {
        obj[k] = processElement(k, v);
    }
}
const timedProcessObject = timerify(processObject);

const obs = new PerformanceObserver((list) => {
    for (const { name, duration } of list.getEntries()) {
        console.log(`Execution of ${name} took ${duration} ms`);
    }

    performance.clearMarks();
    performance.clearMeasures();
    obs.disconnect();
});
obs.observe({ entryTypes: ['function'] });

// warmup (not registered)
parseArray();
parseObject();

const arr = timedParseArray().a;
const obj = timedParseObject().a;

timedProcessArray(arr);
timedProcessObject(obj);
```

</details>

referenced_prs.body:
This PR improves the performance by a few orders of magnitude in my benchmarks, mostly by changing O(n^2) code to something linear. There are 2 separate cases and I can separate one of them into a separate PR for easier review.

## Duplicate keys

This is something that was recently noticed in #539, but there had already been some work in unmerged PR's from @elidoran - #185 and #189.

The [`[].concat(a, b)`](https://github.com/ljharb/qs/blob/6bdfaf5b7c33008ff603af7772844579b42f7a3f/lib/utils.js#L290) makes copies of continually larger arrays every time `combine()` is called. There are (were) tests that check if `combine()` does not mutate input arguments, but 3086902ecf7f088d0d1803887643ac6c03d415b9 introduced new test cases that check if the first (`a`) input is mutated if it is an overflow object. I know that mutating inputs was one of the objections in #185 and #189, but I believe that the performance improvement is large enough to allow it.

This PR does not change `arrayLimit` behaviour, which is wrong (index vs length - #540) and probably should not even be there (https://github.com/ljharb/qs/issues/537#issuecomment-3787759741, #294).

This change should slightly reduce memory usage by making less copies (it reduces the number of allocations, but peak memory usage depends on GC; GHSA-6rw7-vpxm-498p mentions memory exhaustion, but it is not clear how could that happen) and makes the performance comparable to 6.14.1 with `arrayLimit: -1` (in prior versions duplicates are parsed as arrays regardless of `arrayLimit` and `parseArrays` - #543)

<details>
<summary>Memory usage change</summary>

This was tested by parsing a string with 100000 `a[]=b...b` elements, where each `b...b` string had 1000 characters - the input is 100 MB of text. Options: `{ parameterLimit: 100_000, arrayLimit: 100_000 }`

16.4.0 "Allocation" view from "Allocation timeline":

<img width="547" height="328" alt="parseQueryStringValues allocations total count 890151 (live 98345) with size 1474 MB (live 99.9 MB); combine allocations total count 3327 (live 3) with size 5.8 MB (live 168 B)" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/b62e2bdb-486d-4afc-8a1b-235bb965a03c" />

This PR (f8ee66f313bb3f602668eab0a72890f51fcf0ede):

<img width="549" height="428" alt="parseQueryStringValues allocations total count 592909 (live 74896) with size 128 MB (live 76.0 MB); combine allocations total count 17 (live 5) with size 35.4 kB (live 544 B)" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/9a19611d-e61e-4b58-8db6-13e026413acf" />

</details>

My benchmark results are variable, because I have a noisy environment (lots of things open, CPU frequency and core assignment not locked), but the general order of magnitude tells the difference.

| N | 6.14.1 (ms) | PR f8ee66f313bb3f602668eab0a72890f51fcf0ede (ms) |
| - | - | - |
| 100 | 0.48 | 0.20 |
| 1000 | 3.3 | 0.93 |
| 10000 | 220 | 8.7 |
| 100000 | 29576 | 68 |
| 125000 | 45686 | 78 |
| 1000000 | at least 15 minutes, Ctrl+C | call stack size exceeded |

Unfortunately, this may cause regressions with huge arrays, because `Function.apply()` pushes the arguments (all elements of array `b` in this case) to the stack. I don't think that this is a huge problem given that this happens at over 100k elements that previously would be parsed in about ~30 seconds. When exactly will this fail depends on the available size of the stack and may be hard to predict.

<details>
<summary>Benchmark code</summary>

```js
const {
  timerify,
  performance,
  PerformanceObserver,
} = require("node:perf_hooks");
const qs = require(".");

const N = 10_000;
const query = Array(N).fill("a[]=b").join("&");
// const query = Array(N).fill().map((_,i)=>`a[${i}]=b`).join("&");
// const query = Array(N).fill(`a[]=${Array(1000).fill("b").join("")}`).join("&");

function parseArray() {
    return qs.parse(query, {
        parameterLimit: N,
        arrayLimit: N
    });
}
const timedParseArray = timerify(parseArray);

const obs = new PerformanceObserver((list) => {
    for (const { name, duration } of list.getEntries()) {
        console.log(`Execution of ${name} took ${duration} ms`);
    }

    performance.clearMarks();
    performance.clearMeasures();
    obs.disconnect();
});
obs.observe({ entryTypes: ["function"] });

// warmup (not registered)
parseArray();

const arr = timedParseArray().a;
console.assert(Array.isArray(arr), "arr not an array");

```

</details>

Possible input combinations are:

- `a` is an array, `b` is not an array - `a.push(b)`
- `a` is an array, `b` is an array - `a.push.apply(b)`
- `a` is not an array, `b` is not an array - `[].concat(a, b)`
- `a` is not an array, `b` is an array - `[].concat(a, b)` - detected only [once](https://github.com/ljharb/qs/blob/6bdfaf5b7c33008ff603af7772844579b42f7a3f/test/utils.js#L161) in whole test suite, so I don't think `b.unshift(a)` makes sense

This part includes first 2 commits:

- 2870c9ce3388f10e8302741a31995c6c32bcc4ef - replace `.concat()` with `push()` if possible
- 15b1e5efb7a8406b32a51c99d951986ef8ac0cae - allow mutation of the first argument in tests that did not allow it

## Indexed arrays

Here the problem lies in `merge()` and the [`source.forEach()` call](https://github.com/ljharb/qs/blob/6bdfaf5b7c33008ff603af7772844579b42f7a3f/lib/utils.js#L119). When a string like `a[0]=b&a[1]=b&...` is parsed the `merge()` function is called with values of `target` and `source` like those below:

```plain
[ 'b' ] [ <1 empty item>, 'b' ]
[ 'b', 'b' ] [ <2 empty items>, 'b' ]
[ 'b', 'b', 'b' ] [ <3 empty items>, 'b' ]
[ 'b', 'b', 'b', 'b' ] [ <4 empty items>, 'b' ]
```

While the callback of `.forEach()` is executed only for non-empty array items in sparse arrays, it looks like it still is linear with the size of array and not the number of existing items. Since the execution time of `.forEach()` and the number of calls to `merge()` scale with N (number of elements in array with growing indices), this has O(n^2) time complexity.

The idea that I had is iterating over own properties and using the fact that for arrays they always are the indices of elements followed by `'length'`.

Benchmarks use the same code as before, but the second query is uncommented.

| N | 6.14.1 (ms) | PR f8ee66f313bb3f602668eab0a72890f51fcf0ede (ms) |
| - | - | - |
| 100 | 0.30 | 0.31 |
| 1000 | 2.8 | 3.3 |
| 5000 | 224 | 11 |
| 10000 | 911 | 22 |
| 50000 | 22683 | 106 |
| 100000 | 90571 | 221 |
| 1000000 | did not even try | 2193 |

I'm not entirely convinced if this is something worth changing, because for reasonable `arrayLimit` (affects max `.forEach()` execution time) and `parameterLimit` (affects number of `merge()` calls) values the current code performs slightly better. If there are people who change both of those limits, then this may be a real problem.

This part is in the third commit - f8ee66f313bb3f602668eab0a72890f51fcf0ede.

