comments.body:
the app crashes during startup while using 4.20.0. 
The app does not crash while using versions up to 4.19.2.

At some point during InitializeRoutes, using 4.20.0, an exception is thrown in express related codebase module path-to-regexp, function pathToRegexp : 

_path.replace is not a function due to the fact path holds a reference to a class controller instance, not a string ..._

Find two snapshots illustrating the crash, one for the exception thrown, the other one for the variable values while the exception is thrown.

Our basecode is somewhat complex. Here is a full working example. It is using winston a logger framework and can be get ridden of in AbstractService (asbtract base class of every service) and InitializeRoutes

RoutesConfig.json :
```
{
    "routes": [
        {
            "path": "/service",
            "impl": "ServiceController"
        }
}
```

AbstractService.ts :

```
export abstract class AbstractService {
  
    private readonly _logger: winston.Logger;

    /**
     *
     *
     * @param pinstance
     */
    constructor(pinstance: winston.Logger) {
        this._logger = pinstance;
    }

    protected get Logger(): winston.Logger {
        return this._logger;
    }
}
```
RouteLoader.ts :

```
import * as ServiceController from ServiceController;

export class RouteImplLoader {
    private static Annuaire = { ServiceController : ServiceController }

   public getInstance(className: string, ...args: any[]) {
        let resu = null;
        const cimpl = RouteImplLoader.Annuaire[className];
        try {
            resu = new (<any>cimpl)[className](...args);
        } catch (e) {
            resu = null;
        }

        return resu;
    }
}
```

InitializeRoutes.ts :

```
import routesConfig from 'RoutesConfig.json';
import { Express } from 'express';

export class InitializeRoutes extends AbstractService {
    private static LocalLogger = Logger.getLogger();
    public static _routeLoader: RouteImplLoader = new RouteImplLoader();

    public static get RouteLoader() {
        return this._routeLoader;
    }

    /**
     * 
     *
     * @param app : Express
     * @param link : string
     */
    public static Initialize(app: Express, link: string): boolean {
        let resu = true;
        try {
            resu =
                InitializeRoutes.getRoutes(link).map((rc) => {
                    app.use('/', rc.router);
                }).length >= 0;
        } catch (ex) {
            console.log(`InitializeRoutes::Initialize() EXCEPTION !`, ex);
        }
        return resu;
    }

    /**
     *
     * @param link
     * @returns
     */
    public static getRoutes(link: string): BaseStatefullController[] {
        const routes = routesConfig.routes
            .map((route: IRouteDescriptor) => {
                let temp = null;
                try {
                    temp = InitializeRoutes.RouteLoader.getInstance(route.impl, link, route.path);
                } catch (ex) {
                   console.log(
                        `InitializeRoutes::getRoutes() EXCEPTION ${link} !`,
                        ex
                    );
                    temp = null;
                }
                return temp;
            })
            .filter((route) => {
                return !!route;
            }) as BaseStatefullController[];

        return routes;
    }
}

```



Main entry point : 


```
let expr = express();
expr.set('env', ServerConfig.env);
InitializeRoutes.Initialize(expr, 'http://127.0.0.1:9999');
let _server = http.createServer(this._app);
expr.listen(9999, '127.0.0.1', () => {
                console.log('ready, awaiting queries ...');
            });
```

![express4 20 0_bug](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/310b08ef-c4b4-47a7-83d4-68eefdd8b13c)

![express variables 4 20 0_bug](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/885f2718-8d6e-4486-bd6e-cc7ac0f78a3d)

We have completed a code review early this morning : the way the middleware layer set up controllers counterveine the way newer express version (>=4.20.0) binds route paths to controller instances due to a bug in call to bind method.

The team that reviewed middleware code portion implemented a patch that way :

Every controllers inherits a base class that binds express 'Post' Verbs to the path and instance :

Buggy way to bind the path to the instance that do not work anymore with 4.20.0 and +  :
```
public async InitializePost() {
        this.router.post(this.path, this.processService.bind(this)).bind(**this**);
}
```

The implemented fix that fix things up : 

```
public async InitializePost() {
        this.router.post(this.path, this.processService.bind(this)).bind(**this.path**);
}
```

@Number16BusShelter I had a similar issue. I've tried downgrading to 1.5.0 like you suggest but that didn't help.
Found this https://github.com/nodejs/node/issues/42116 issue in the node repo, downgraded to 16.3.0 and it worked like a charm without any errors. I'm using ESM and apparently they broke something in 16.4+.
Of course, downgrading node is not the best option, I'll investigate further when I have time.

FYI, this comes from the [parameterize](https://github.com/knex/knex/blob/3475d8166863d6c9535527cd541d7c894e21ea84/lib/client.js#L404) function in the client.

From 
```
// json columns can have object in values.
if (isPlainObject(value)) {
  value = JSON.stringify(value);
}
```

To:

```js
// json columns can have object in values.
if (isPlainObject(value) || Array.isArray(value)) {
  value = JSON.stringify(value);
}
```

I suppose that the intent of the fix was to allow JSON columns having arrays (as it's totally legit), but without introspection (or another hint), it's not possible to distinguish legit array columns from JSON columns with an array value.

I submitted a new PR to add a check for array containing plain object rather than just do an Array.isArray check https://github.com/knex/knex/pull/5444

```ts
// check for plain object and array containing at least one plain object
if (isPlainObject(value) || (Array.isArray(value) && value.some(isPlainObject))) {
   value = JSON.stringify(value);
}
```

