@kavanet 
*Some developers are claiming that loading .json is causing some troubles when bundling with webpack.* - this is pretty vague. I bet most of the mentioned "problems" are in fact same problem as here - that whole JSON file gets included when imported into an app...

@DigitalBrainJS 
There is a alternative (and recommended by Webpack devs) way how to include version field from `package.json` and that is the use of DefinePlugin - see [this issue](https://github.com/webpack/webpack/issues/237)

Added benefit of DefinePlugin is that it just replaces the "variable" in the code with the constant (string "0.24.4" here) instead of JS variable. This allows automatic dead code elimination in later build stages (Terser)

@kawanet 
1) I've only optimized browser build, and as you said since the builders don't load the `http` adapter for browser builds, it doesn't make much sense to load `env / data.json`. But we can do this anyway in order to have one shared configuration.
2) Yes, I thought about JS config file, but JSON format is more natural for [generated] configs, moreover, it's a bit simpler to write JSON data to file from the code. Yes, webpack has a strange JSON loader, unlike rollup that imports JSON as an object without JSON.parse in the generated code. All popular builders work fine with JSON. Anyway, I'm going to refactor the code for using js just due to the ugly JSON.parse generating by webpack for JSON imports.

