For future reference: one change with this approach is that components rendered in `<Head/>` can’t rely on context being passed to them (but should gracefully support a likely future version of Next that starts providing it) on the client *or* server.

Instead, you should explicitly bridge any needed context in the meantime:

```jsx
function HeadWithContext({ children }) {
  const foo = React.useContext(FooContext)
  const bar = React.useContext(BarContext)

  return (
    <Head>
      <FooContext.Provider value={foo}>
        <BarContext.Provider value={bar}>
          {children}
        </BarContext.Provider>
      </FooContext.Provider>
    </Head>
  )
}
```

@timneutkens sorry to directly ping you, but [es-modules-support definitely seems to be the culprit](https://nextjs.org/blog/next-11-1#es-modules-support) and I don't understand why. Could you please shed some light onto what changed in Next12? Y'all said Next12 is "backwards compatible" but it seems that is not the case with enabling by default instead of opt-in:

> ES Modules support includes backward compatibility to support the previous import behavior of [CommonJS](https://nodejs.org/docs/latest/api/modules.html). In Next.js 12, `esmExternals: true` will become the default

That doesn't seem like a backwards compat change given the amount of mem leaks people are running into 🤷🏻 But maybe I am misunderstanding. Setting to false "works" so I can now run Next >12.0.1 (including latest), but is that the right thing to do? Again, what are we disabling exactly and why do we want to do this? I don't want to do this just cause "now it works" I'd like to understand any tradeoffs. Thanks!

