issues.body:
# Bug report

## Describe the bug

When using a custom server, the API `app.serveStatic(req, res, filePath)` is used to return static files, but  the server returns a `404` error page, even though the file is present on disk.

I'm using a custom `server.js` to run next and serve a service worker config produced at runtime by `next-offline`.

After upgrading from `9.3.1` to `9.3.2`, the file cannot be served as the server returns a `404`.

## To Reproduce

With the following `server.js`:
```js
const fs = require("fs")
const { createServer } = require("http")
const { join } = require("path")
const { parse } = require("url")

const next = require("next")

const app = next({ dev: process.env.NODE_ENV !== "production" })
const handle = app.getRequestHandler()

app.prepare().then(() => {
  createServer((req, res) => {
    const parsedUrl = parse(req.url, true)
    const { pathname } = parsedUrl

    if (pathname === "/service-worker.js") {
      const filePath = join(__dirname, ".next", pathname)
      console.log({
        filePath,
        exists: fs.existsSync(filePath),
        files: fs.readdirSync(join(__dirname, ".next")),
      })

      app.serveStatic(req, res, filePath)
    } else {
      handle(req, res, parsedUrl)
    }
  }).listen(3000, () => {
    console.log(`> Ready on http://localhost:${3000}`)
  })
})
```

in your shell run
```
rm -rf node_modules .next
yarn add next@9.3.1
node server.js
```

The file content is correctly served at `http://localhost:3000/service-worker.js`

Then run:
```
rm -rf node_modules .next
yarn add next@9.3.2
node server.js
```
The url `http://localhost:3000/service-worker.js` returns a `404` and shows the default error page.


## Expected behavior

The file should be served consistently accross versions

## Screenshots
N/A

## System information

- OS: linux ubuntu 18.04
- Browser (if applies): any
- Version of Next.js: 9.3.2

## Additional context

Here is the `console.log` extract from `server.js` showing that the file is present when the `404` is returned:
```
{ filePath:
   '/pathtomyproject/.next/service-worker.js',
  exists: true,
  files:
   [ 'build-manifest.json',
     'cache',
     'react-loadable-manifest.json',
     'server',
     'service-worker.js',
     'static' ] }
```

## Bug Description

Since the fix for GHSA-35p6-xmwp-9g52 (v7.28.0 / v8.5.0 / v6.27.0), every request dispatched onto a reused idle keep-alive socket is deferred behind a `setTimeout(..., 0)` in `scheduleIdleSocketValidation` (`lib/dispatcher/client-h1.js`). Node's timer floor makes that ~1ms+, which turns sub-0.1ms keep-alive requests into ~1.4ms requests for any sequential (poll-style) workload — a ~15x latency regression on low-RTT links.

To be clear: this is **not** a request to remove the response-queue-poisoning protection. It's a request to reduce the cost of the validation tick.

## Reproducible By

```js
// node bench.mjs  (npm i undici@7.27.2 / undici@8.6.0 to compare)
import { createServer } from 'node:http';
import { Agent, fetch } from 'undici';

const server = createServer((req, res) => {
    res.writeHead(200, { 'content-type': 'application/json' });
    res.end('{"ok":1}');
});
server.keepAliveTimeout = 65000;
server.listen(0, '127.0.0.1', async () => {
    const url = 'http://127.0.0.1:' + server.address().port + '/';
    const agent = new Agent({ keepAliveTimeout: 60000, connections: 256, pipelining: 1 });
    for (let i = 0; i < 300; i++) { await (await fetch(url, { dispatcher: agent })).text(); } // warmup
    const times = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < 2000; i++) {
        const t0 = process.hrtime.bigint();
        await (await fetch(url, { dispatcher: agent })).text();
        times.push(Number(process.hrtime.bigint() - t0));
    }
    times.sort((a, b) => a - b);
    console.log('sequential p50:', (times[1000] / 1e6).toFixed(3), 'ms');
    await agent.close();
    server.close();
});
```

Measured (node v22.22.1, linux x64, loopback, median of interleaved runs):

| version | sequential p50 | 8-wide burst p50 |
|---|---|---|
| 7.27.2 (pre-fix) | 0.078–0.083 ms | 0.49–0.52 ms |
| 7.28.0 | 1.385 ms | 1.95 ms |
| 8.6.0 | 1.36–1.41 ms | 1.91–1.97 ms |

Bursts pay the tick once per reused idle socket per round.

## Expected Behavior

Idle-socket liveness validation with (sub-)microsecond-scale overhead in the common case where the socket is healthy.

The goal of the tick, as I read it, is to give the event loop a chance to surface an unsolicited response / FIN / RST that arrived while the socket sat idle, before a new request is associated with it. Some candidate directions that keep that property without the ~1ms timer floor:

- run the deferred resume off `setImmediate` instead of `setTimeout(0)` — the check phase still runs after the poll phase, so pending `'data'`/`'end'` events on the idle socket are processed first, at ~50µs instead of ~1ms+;
- skip (or fast-path) validation when the socket has been idle for less than some small threshold, by analogy with #5400's fresh-socket guard — a socket that went idle microseconds ago on a `pipelining: 1` client has had no meaningful window to receive an injected response;
- an explicit opt-in fast mode for clients that only talk to trusted upstreams was considered and discarded here — an option that silently reopens GHSA-35p6-xmwp-9g52 seems worse than making the mitigation cheap.

Context: found while migrating [ccxt](https://github.com/ccxt/ccxt) (exchange-polling workloads, latency-sensitive users on single-digit-ms RTT links) from node-fetch to undici — node core's `http.Agent` achieves the same safety by destroying idle sockets that receive unsolicited data, with no per-request deferral, so undici currently benchmarks slower than the `node-fetch`+`https.Agent` stack it replaces on these workloads. Happy to test patches or contribute a PR if maintainers agree on a direction.

## Environment

node v22.22.1, Linux x64; undici 7.27.2 / 7.28.0 / 8.6.0 from npm

## Additional context

Related merged work touching the same code path: #5397 / #5402 / #5407 / #5409 (fake-timer interop), #5400 (fresh-socket guard, v6.x backport).


## Disclosure

This issue (analysis, benchmarks, and text) was created by Claude (model: claude-fable), an AI assistant by Anthropic, operating under human direction. Please review accordingly.

comments.body:
Ran into this independently after a Node 26.3.0 → 26.3.1 upgrade made concurrent `fetch()` POSTs to the same origin execute sequentially, and root-caused it before finding this issue — posting the analysis here rather than opening a duplicate. Hopefully this unblocks triage: it confirms @wgoodall01's diagnosis and pins it to two specific code locations.

## Mechanism

Two pieces interact:

**1. The stream-body guard in `lib/dispatcher/client-h2.js` → `busy(request)`** only lets a request with a stream / async-iterable / FormData body dispatch when zero requests are running on the session:

```js
if (util.bodyLength(request.body) !== 0 &&
  (util.isStream(request.body) || util.isAsyncIterable(request.body) || util.isFormDataLike(request.body))) return true
```

`fetch()` always hands its body to dispatch as a stream/async iterable, so **every body-carrying `fetch()` hits this guard**, regardless of body size (1 byte reproduces it). `request()` with a buffer body does not — which is exactly why #5391 fixed `request()` POSTs but not `fetch()` POSTs.

**2. Since v8.4.0, `Client[kBusy]` (`lib/dispatcher/client.js`) no longer reports pending requests as busy once an h2 context is attached** (the `allowsMux` / `getMaxConcurrent` change), so `Pool`/`Agent` never opens additional connections for queued requests.

On ≤ 8.3.0 the same stream-body guard existed, but the busy signal made the pool spawn one connection per pending request — parallelism survived via connection fan-out (wasteful, but fast). From 8.4.0 the fan-out is gone, so the guard drains body-carrying requests strictly **one per response round-trip**: N parallel POSTs ≈ N × request latency.

## Version matrix

50 parallel 128 KB POSTs, local h2 server with a 20 ms handler delay, wall-clock:

| undici | wall | behavior |
|---|---|---|
| 8.3.0 | 68 ms | parallel (pool fan-out, ~50 connections) |
| 8.4.0 | 1250 ms | serialized |
| 8.4.1 | 1223 ms | serialized |
| 8.5.0 | 1200 ms | serialized |
| 8.6.0 | 1182 ms | serialized |
| **8.7.0 (latest)** | 1185 ms | serialized |
| 8.5.0 with the guard removed | **38 ms** | parallel on a single multiplexed session |

The last row is the smoking-gun experiment: deleting the two guard lines from 8.5.0 restores full parallelism (faster than 8.3.0, since one multiplexed session beats 50 TLS handshakes). I'm not proposing that as the fix as-is — a stream body genuinely can't be replayed on retry — but it isolates the cause to that guard alone. Two fix directions that seem consistent with the codebase:

- multiplex stream-body requests and mark them non-retryable (retry eligibility is already evaluated per-request in `canRetryRequestAfterGoAway`, and the v8.5.0 removal of the idempotency serialization used exactly this reasoning); or
- keep the guard but have the h2 client report busy to the pool when the guard defers a request, restoring the ≤ 8.3.0 connection fan-out for stream bodies.

Since this ships in Node (26.3.0 bundles 8.3.0 = OK; 26.3.1 / 26.4.0 bundle 8.5.0 = affected), the regression lands via a semver-patch Node upgrade — easy to miss in review.

