One other thing @ryandiginomad, I think we can actually improve the test by making it more similar to the reported GH issue. Summary here:

The e2e test builds the unevenly-loaded array with `delete (order!.lines[1].productVariant as any).product`, so it verifies the fix against a hand-constructed tree rather than one any code path produces. It would keep passing even if nothing ever generated that shape.

The shape is reachable through the public API. Core's own line-append path yields `[missing, present]` (a newly added line's variant carries `product` and is pushed last), which `master` already handles because it samples `lines[0]`. The `[present, missing]` ordering comes from a plugin hydrating a *single* element in place — the `OrderInterceptor` pattern documented in `order-interceptor.ts:168`, and what the reporter described in #4537.

Suggested replacement:

```ts
it('hydrates lines after a plugin hydrated one line\'s variant', async () => {
    await shopClient.asAnonymousUser();
    await shopClient.query(addItemToOrderDocument, { productVariantId: 'T_1', quantity: 1 });
    const { addItemToOrder } = await shopClient.query(addItemToOrderDocument, {
        productVariantId: 'T_5',
        quantity: 1,
    });
    orderResultGuard.assertSuccess(addItemToOrder);

    const internalOrderId = +addItemToOrder.id.replace(/^\D+/g, '');
    const ctx = await server.app.get(RequestContextService).create({ apiType: 'admin' });
    const hydrator = server.app.get(EntityHydrator);
    const order = await server.app
        .get(OrderService)
        .findOne(ctx, internalOrderId, ['lines.productVariant']);

    expect(order!.lines[0].productVariant.product).toBeUndefined();
    expect(order!.lines[1].productVariant.product).toBeUndefined();

    // A plugin acts on one line and hydrates just that line's variant.
    await hydrator.hydrate(ctx, order!.lines[0].productVariant, { relations: ['product'] });

    // The array is now unevenly loaded: [present, missing].
    expect(order!.lines[0].productVariant.product).toBeDefined();
    expect(order!.lines[1].productVariant.product).toBeUndefined();

    await hydrator.hydrate(ctx, order!, { relations: ['lines.productVariant.product'] });

    expect(order!.lines[0].productVariant.product).toBeDefined();
    expect(order!.lines[1].productVariant.product).toBeDefined();
    expect(order!.lines[0].productVariant.product.id).toBe(order!.lines[0].productVariant.productId);
    expect(order!.lines[1].productVariant.product.id).toBe(order!.lines[1].productVariant.productId);
});
```

Differences: the uneven state is produced by a real `hydrate()` call rather than by editing the entity, the two intermediate assertions pin that the shape actually arose, and `productVariant.productId` replaces the hardcoded `toBe(1)`/`toBe(2)` so the test isn't coupled to fixture CSV row order or the id strategy.

Verified locally: fails on `master`, passes on this branch, full `entity-hydrator.e2e-spec.ts` 26/26.

@biggamesmallworld Appreciate you writing out the full replacement and verifying it — adopted it in 236ecba96.

The `delete`-based original built a [present, missing] shape no code path produces, so it would have kept passing even if the fix regressed. Driving the uneven state through a real hydrate() on a single line's variant is a genuine regression guard, and comparing each product.id to that line's own productId drops the coupling to fixture row order / id strategy.

Verified locally (sqljs): on master the new test fails at `expect(order.lines[1].productVariant.product).toBeDefined()` — after the whole-order hydrate, the second line's product stays undefined because the walker only samples lines[0] — and it passes on this branch, with entity-hydrator.e2e-spec.ts green.

There seems to be a similar issue actually in 16.1.7. When navigating back from an external site, things seem to work fine... at least at first.  But no useEffects or useLayoutEffects are firing at all. 

You cant see any of this in development, you need to `yarn build` and `yarn start`, making sure to set some kind of state before navigating away to an external site. When clicking the back button, that state will not change if you have a useEffect that changes it. 

For now, I’ve added a script tag after the opening `<body>` tag, setting a session cookie when navigating away, and then clearing it and doing a hard reload.

```
<script
					dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{
						__html: '
							window.addEventListener("pageshow", function (e) {
								try {
									const pending = sessionStorage.getItem("stripe_redirect_pending") === "1"

									if (pending) {
										sessionStorage.removeItem("stripe_redirect_pending")
										window.location.reload()
									}
								} catch (err) {}
							});
						`
					}}
				/>
```

